**Basic version**
The core components are usually:
- Renewable generation (solar panels, wind turbines)
- Power distribution (distribution boards, substations)
- Monitoring and control (PLCs, SCADA systems, remote monitoring)
- Communication links (smart meters, remote terminal units, industrial automation)
- Cybersecurity layers (security monitoring, intrusion detection)

**Advanced version**
More integration means more attack surface. Smart grid vulnerabilities include:
- Default credentials and weak authentication (many utilities still use weak passwords or default admin credentials)
- Outdated software and firmware (many commercial-grade devices still running outdated operating systems)
- Physical tampering (unauthorized access to substations, transformer banks)
- Insecure communications (over-the-air firmware updates, weak encryption)
- Supply chain risks (vendor dependencies, third-party components)
- Human error (lateral movement from internal network)

